<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vercingetorix</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 82–<a href="/years/46-bce">46 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vercingetorix was a chief of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arverni">Arverni</a> who in 52 BCE united most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a> in revolt against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a>. He used scorched-earth tactics and repulsed Caesar at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gergovia">Gergovia</a>, but was besieged at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alesia-city">Alesia</a>, the town of the Mandubii, and surrendered when a relief army failed. He was displayed in Caesar's triumph in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and executed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vercingetorix.jpg" alt="Vercingetorix" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vercing%C3%A9torix_stat%C3%A8re_Gallica_avers.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Vercingetorix belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arverni">Arverni</a>, a people of what is now Auvergne. Their king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bituitus">Bituitus</a> was decisively defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 121 BCE, and the Arverni later abandoned kingship for an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oligarchy">oligarchy</a> like that of their rivals the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aedui">Aedui</a>. Vercingetorix was probably born at their chief town <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gergovia">Gergovia</a>. His birth year is inferred from Caesar calling him a young man in 52 BCE, and scholars place it in the 80s or 70s BCE. His father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/celtillus">Celtillus</a> was murdered by fellow aristocrats, around 70 to 60 BCE, for trying to restore kingship. His name probably means supreme king of warriors and may be a title rather than a birth name. Nearly all that is known comes from Caesar, who describes him through a Roman lens.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In early 52 BCE, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carnutes">Carnutes</a> massacred <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Cenabum</a>, Vercingetorix tried to raise the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arverni">Arverni</a>, but his uncle Gobannitio and other aristocrats exiled him. He rallied countryside followers, retook <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gergovia">Gergovia</a> and was proclaimed king. About ten peoples joined, and he imposed harsh discipline. When Caesar took Vellaunodunum, Cenabum and Noviodunum, Vercingetorix adopted scorched-earth tactics, but the Romans stormed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avaricum">Avaricum</a>, where only about 800 of the reported inhabitants escaped. At Gergovia he drove Caesar back, one of only three recorded Roman defeats of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallic-wars">Gallic Wars</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aedui">Aedui</a> then joined him, and an assembly at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bibracte">Bibracte</a> made him sole commander.</p><p>A cavalry defeat sent him into the Mandubian town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alesia-city">Alesia</a>, which Caesar surrounded with siege works. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a> expelled women, children and the old, whom Caesar refused to admit; they starved between the lines. A relief army failed in three assaults, and Vercingetorix accepted responsibility, saying the war had been fought for collective freedom, and offered himself to spare his people.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> has him ride out in fine armour, circle Caesar and sit at his feet; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassius-dio">Cassius Dio</a>, thought more plausible, says he hoped for pardon because of past friendship with Caesar. He was held six years in the Tullianum, shown in Caesar's triumph in 46 BCE and executed there, possibly by strangulation. Zecchini suggests Caesar could not appear pro-Gallic while Roman opinion was hostile to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a>.</p><p>Largely forgotten in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, Vercingetorix was rediscovered in the 16th century. In 19th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, especially after 1871, he became a symbol of national identity and of the noble loser. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a> financed excavations at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alesia-city">Alesia</a> and commissioned a bronze statue there by Aimé Millet. Painters such as Lionel Royer in 1899 fixed the image of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> throwing down his weapons.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vercingetorix” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Between 58 and 50 BCE Julius Caesar conquered the rest of Gaul; <b>Vercingetorix</b> of the Arverni united most Gauls against him in 52 BCE and held Gergovia, but surrendered at Alesia.</blockquote><a href="/culture/celts">Celts · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>Peoples were divided into tribes, usually ruled by kings, and allied tribes could choose a war leader, as an assembly of Gauls at Bibracte chose <b>Vercingetorix</b> against Rome.</blockquote><a href="/culture/la-tene-culture">La Tène culture · Society</a></li><li><blockquote>Gaul as a whole stayed divided, and only in a time of crisis, Caesar’s invasion, did the Gauls unite under one leader, the Arvernian chieftain <b>Vercingetorix</b>, in 52 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/societies/gaul">Social Hierarchy of Ancient Gaul</a></li><li><blockquote>Pressed by the Sequani and Ariovistus, the Aedui called on Caesar in 58 BCE, but in 52 BCE they joined <b>Vercingetorix</b>, who was chosen supreme leader at Bibracte.</blockquote><a href="/state/aedui">Aedui</a></li><li><blockquote>After the defeat of Bituitus kingship was abolished, and Celtillus, father of <b>Vercingetorix</b>, was put to death for seeking to restore it.</blockquote><a href="/state/arverni">Arverni</a></li><li><blockquote>In 52 BCE they joined <b>Vercingetorix</b> and burned some twenty of their own towns to starve the Romans, but spared Avaricum, which Caesar took after a hard siege, killing most of its inhabitants.</blockquote><a href="/state/bituriges-cubi">Bituriges Cubi</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=-59" aria-label="Rome on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4854 672 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Vercingetorix"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5304 942h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=-59">What was here in 59 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
